      "source": "(z'/z)/(P'/P) equals the leading closed form -2/(h*z*tan alpha) to 1.09 % — measured 369.74358, closed form 365.7473. The residual is exactly the dropped 2*alpha*h'/h^2 term in dz/dq; asserted as a ratio because the witness tolerance is absolute. Note the sign: alpha < 0 at every substrate anchor, so the leading minus is what makes the inflation positive. The factor is large because alpha is small, which is forced by the ladder's correction being small, so slope/value in the z coordinate is not a coordinate-invariant measure of fine-tuning and cannot discriminate between candidate phases. Scope-(b) for that claim (it is exact algebra). Does NOT rescue the (z-1)h/2 family, which stays excluded by the opposite-sign slopes recorded in qbeta-ladder-required-phase.witness.json."
